{
  "raw": {
    "https://careereducation.wellesley.edu/elr": {
      "title": "Experiential Learning",
      "description": "Experiential Learning at Wellesley | Wellesley Career Education Skip to main content MyWellesley Give Handshake Hive Events ELR Getting Started Employers Faculty Parents About Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Experiential Learning at Wellesley Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Handshake Hive Events Deadlines Resources Recruit About Home Experiential Learning at Wellesley Experiential Learning at Wellesley At Wellesley College, students develop the skills and aptitudes to thrive and translate the liberal arts to the professional world, no matter what occupation or career they choose. The faculty work in partnership with Career Education to offer hundreds of unique experiential opportunities that connect classroom learning and professional aspirations to ensure all students engage in the transformative integrative learning that leads to meaningful post-graduate lives and careers. Experiential Learning Browser Link for Students to Submit for Experiential Learning Units (domestic students) F1 International Students only: Link to Pre-Register for Experiential Learning Units The Experiential Learning Requirement Effective for students entering in fall of 2024 and beyond, each candidate for the degree of Bachelor of Arts shall complete two units of experiential learning prior to the start of the seventh semester. Two units will typically be completed in one summer-long or two semester-long experiences. These units can be fulfilled through internships, research, civic engagement, select student employment positions, and other experiences that meet the criteria below. Although only two units are required, students should complete and record as many units of experiential learning as desired to achieve their career and educational goals. Students can meet with their Advisor for Career Exploration (ACE) for support with the ELR by making an appointment in Handshake. Please note that transfer students who enter with sophomore standing or later must only complete one experiential learning unit. *The two experiential learning units are in addition to the 32 academic units required for the Wellesley degree. Please note that academic priorities should always take precedence over experiential learning opportunities. Experiences that earn academic units may not be double-counted as experiential learning units. Opportunities in conjunction with academic units, such as research in the context of a course or independent study, also cannot count for experiential learning units. Opportunities Hundreds of experiential learning opportunities are available to you as a Wellesley College student. Beyond our campus, self-identified opportunities (such as internships and research roles) may also fulfill the requirement. Two units will typically be completed in one summer-long or two semester-long experiences and are defined by the hour expectations below: 1 experiential learning unit: 50–150 hours (in one semester or summer) 2 experiential learning units: 151+ hours (in one semester or summer) Students can search the College’s experiential learning browser for opportunities to fulfill this requirement. The main categories to fufill the requirement are listed below. Certain programs will be automatically added to student transcripts. Students participating in programs on this list do not need to fill out the application form. Career Education will connect with your program administrator following the experience to accurately record your engagement. Internships & Research Internships & research opportunities can confirm a professional interest, open a new door, or lead you in an unexpected direction. They can help you build confidence and understand yourself and your role in the world — academically, professionally, and personally. The College offers over 300 funded opportunities for internship & research each year, and self-identified positions may also be eligible. Eligible Experiences Wellesley-Funded Summer Internships (2 units), includes: Career Education & Departmental Placements Internships funded by Career Education Grants Luce Asia Internship Program Davis Museum Internship Program Botanic Gardens Internship Program Lumpkin Nonprofit Internship Program Wellesley in Washington Summer Internship Program Albright Fellow Program (2 units) Short Internship Project (SHIPS program) (1 unit) Science Center Summer Research Program (2 units) Science Center Sophomore Early Research Program (2 units) Science Center First-Year Apprentice Program (1 unit) Wintersession Biochemistry Research Week (1 unit) Social Science Summer Research Program (2 units) Ronald E. McNair Post-Baccalaureate Achievement Program (2 units) Self-Identified Internship or Research Position (1–2 units) Career Education Upskill Program (1 unit) Fellowships There are select undergraduate fellowship opportunities that provide experiential learning in the form of research or internships. Eligible Experiences Mellon Mays Undergraduate Fellowship (1–2 units) Projects for Peace Fellowship (1–2 units) Self-Identified Fellowship supporting an internship or research opportunity (1–2 units) Student Employment On-campus student employment, managed by Wellesley Human Resources, is an opportunity to develop career readiness and grow professionally. Student jobs are eligible to receive Experiential Learning Unit only in cases where students can articulate how the experience (1) informed their career goals, (2) helped them to build new skills, and (3) supported them in developing an understanding of their strengths/values. More Students can search for student employment opportunities in Workday. Approved employment opportunities are eligible for 1 or 2 units, depending on the hours worked. Student Co-op leaders who are advised by the Director of the BEAM initiative and Saxby’s Student Cafe Operating Officers are eligible for units. Please note that opportunities that are supervised by an actively enrolled undergraduate peer are not elibile (such as opportunities where you work within a campus co-op and report directly to a peer). Community Service and Civic Engagement Civic engagement supports students in exploring the role of social justice, community engagement, and leadership across all majors and career fields. During the fall and spring semesters, there are a wide range of opportunities for sustained, collaborative, reflective, and meaningful ways to engage that enrich and support a liberal arts education while partnering with fellow students, communities, and organizations. Eligible Experiences Community Service (1–2 units) Service Journey Spring Break Trips (1 unit, participant or peer leader) Ministrare Peer Leader (1–2 units) Clinton Fellow Program (1–2 units) Civic Action Lab Team Leader (1 unit) Experiential Learning Advising If you are seeking support in identifying opportunities, or want to discuss experiential learning possibilities, we encourage you to make an appointment in Handshake to meet with your Advisor for Career Exploration (ACE). Hayley Meredith is the ACE for the class years of 2028 and 2030. Ariane Baker is the ACE for the class years of 2027 and 2029. Ariane Baker Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2027 & 2029) Hayley Meredith Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2028 & 2030) Process for Experience Submission and Unit Recording on Transcript Experiential learning units are recorded by semester on a section of the transcript separate from academic course units titled “Experiential Learning.” Students do not pre-register for their units unless they are F-1 International students. Submission for recording only happens once the unit(s) have been completed. Experiential learning units are awarded on a semester-by-semester basis. You cannot submit for multiple semesters simulataneously; the form must be completed only for one semester at a time. Deadlines to submit for experiential learning units are as follows: For Spring semester (Jan. 20 – May 30, 2026): deadline July 1 For Summer term (Apr. 1 – Aug. 20, 2026): deadline Oct. 1 For Fall semester (Aug. 31 – Dec. 9, 2026): deadline Feb. 1 For Wintersession (Jan 4 – Jan. 21, 2027): deadline Mar.1 For year-long, part-time experiences (such as student employment or research assistant roles), each part-time semester of work is eligible for one experiential learning unit. The form should be submitted following each semester of work. Any questions should be emailed to ELR@wellesley.edu . FAQs What programs are exempt from the application process (automatically added to student transcripts)? Certain programs will be automatically added to student transcripts. Students participating in programs on this list do not need to fill out the application form. Career Education will connect with your program administrator following the experience to accurately record your engagement. Who is required to complete the ELR? Students who entered Wellesley in Fall 2024 and beyond are required to complete two experiential learning units prior to the start of the seventh semester in order to graduate. Students who entered prior to Fall 2024 may submit the form to have their experiential learning reflected on their transcript, but are not required to do so. Transfer students who enter with sophomore standing or later must only complete one experiential learning unit. How many units are required prior to graduation? Two experiential learning units need to be completed prior to the seventh semester. All students who entered the College in fall 2024 or later fall within this new degree requirement. Students who began their studies prior to fall 2024, are not required to complete 2 units but are able to record these experiences on their transcript if they desire to do so. Transfer students who enter with sophomore standing or later must only complete one experiential learning unit. Experiential Learning Units are defined as below: 2 units: 151+ hours of experiential learning 1 unit: 50 - 150 hours of experiential learning The two-unit ELR requirement will typically be completed in one summer-long or two fall- or spring-long experiences. When do I need to complete this requirement by? Students who entered Wellesley in fall 2024 and beyond must complete the requirement prior to the start of the seventh semester. Transfer students who enter with sophomore standing or later must only complete one experiential learning unit. What counts toward this requirement? This requirement may be fulfilled through experiences such as: internships, research, civic engagement, and select student employment roles. Experiences must consist of applied learning intended to enhance professional skills or knowledge through firsthand experiences beyond the academic classroom, and Include a third party employer/supervisor who oversaw the experience. The experience supervisor may not be an immediate family member, first degree relative or actively enrolled undergraduate peer (such as working within a campus co-op and reporting directly to a peer). Experiential learning units are awarded on a semester-by-semester basis. You cannot submit for multiple semesters simulataneously; the form must be completed only for one semester/period at a time. 2 experiential learning units: 151+ hours of experiential learning (during one semester/period) 1 experiential learning unit: 50 - 150 hours of experiential learning (during one semester/period) How can I find opportunities? A great place to start looking for experiential opportunities most relevant to your studies and career aspirations is the Wellesley College Experiential Learning Browser . This includes a variety of Wellesley-specific opportunities such as funded internships, research programs, civic engagement opportunities, and fellowships. For student employment opportunities, please visit the job board in Workday. This will display only positions available to immediately apply for. Please check frequently throughout the year for additional opportunities. Please note that not all student employment opportunities are eligible for experiential learning units. Opportunities must include at least 50 hours of relevant experiences where students focus on an active learning process and build skills relevant to their future aspirations. The role cannot be supervised by an actively enrolled undergraduate peer (such as working within a campus co-op and reporting directly to a peer). Check with your ACE if you have questions about whether an opportunity would apply. There are summer internships and research opportunities offered by organizations around the world for college students. Thousands of opportunities are available to apply to through Handshake . Not all opportunities in Handshake will complete the ELR. Check with your ACE if you have questions about whether an opportunity would apply. Advisors in Career Education are available to support students year round in preparing applications and interviewing for opportunities. What if I am unsure whether my experience fulfills the ELR requirement? Please reach out to ELR@wellesley.edu with information about the experience or schedule an appointment to meet with your Advisor for Career Exploration (ACE) through Handshake . Can I record more than 2 units of experiential learning on my transcript? Yes! Students can record as many units of experiential learning as they complete during their time at Wellesley and would like visible on their transcript. However, students can submit for a maximum of 2 unique experiential learning opportunities per semester or summer/winter term. How many experiential learning opportunities can I record during a given semester/term? Students are eligible to submit for a maximum of 2 unique experiential learning opportunities per semester or summer/winter term. What is the process for getting my experience approved? Please note: F-1 international students must follow a separate process; see FAQ below. Students must complete and submit the Experiential Learning Submission Form . Deadlines to submit for experiential learning units are as follows: For Spring semester (Jan. 20 – May 30, 2026): deadline July 1 For Summer term (Apr. 1 – Aug. 20, 2026): deadline Oct. 1 For Fall semester (Aug. 31 – Dec. 9, 2026): deadline Feb. 1 For Wintersession (Jan 4 – Jan. 21, 2027): deadline Mar.1 For year-long, part-time experiences (such as student employment or research assistant roles), each part-time semester of work is eligible for one experiential learning unit. The form should be submitted following each semester of work . Information submitted will be sent first to Career Education for approval then to a relevant supervisor or employer for verification. Please allow 2–4 weeks processing time. You will receive updates via email throughout the process as the status of your application changes. You may also view the status of your application at any time by visiting Workday. Any questions should be emailed to ELR@wellesley.edu . I am an F-1 international student. What is the process for getting my experience approved? F-1 international students must complete and submit the Experiential Learning Pre-Registration Form prior to beginning their experiential learning opportunity. This form is required in advance of all ELR experiences. Please use this form to pre-register for all planned Spring Experiential Learning — including paid or unpaid internships / research, volunteer positions, or student employment — before beginning the experience. The Slater Center staff will work with all students who submit this form to determine whether Curricular Practical Training (CPT) off-campus work authorization is required for the experience. At the end of each semester, F1 international students may submit the Experiential Learning Submission Form for all experiences for which they were pre-approved. Deadlines to submit the Experiential Learning Units Post-Form are as follows: For Spring semester (Jan. 20 – May 30, 2026): deadline July 1 For Summer term (Apr. 1 – Aug. 20, 2026): deadline Oct. 1 For Fall semester (Aug. 31 – Dec. 9, 2026): deadline Feb. 1 For Wintersession (Jan 4 – Jan. 21, 2027): deadline Mar.1 For year-long, part-time experiences (such as student employment or research assistant roles), each part-time semester of work is eligible for one experiential learning unit. Information submitted will be sent first to Career Education for approval then to a relevant supervisor or employer for verification. Please allow 2–4 weeks processing time. You will receive updates via email throughout the process as the status of your application changes. You may also view the status of your application at any time by visiting Workday. Any questions should be emailed to ELR@wellesley.edu . Do the 2 experiential learning units count toward my 32 academic units? No. The 2 experiential learning units are in addition to the 32 academic units. Experiences that earn academic units may not be double-counted as experiential learning units. Can I earn experiential learning units for opportunities taken while on a leave of absence? No. Students must be actively working towards their degree requirement for an experience to be eligible for an EL unit. Students cannot submit for experiential learning units for opportunities they had during a leave of absence. Please note: If a student takes a leave of absence for a spring semester, their status with the college will not return to \"active\" until the following fall semester. Therefore, students cannot record EL units for experiences completed the summer following a spring leave of absence. My internship employer has requested a Internship Agreement signature. Who can sign this form for me? Please email all Employer Internship/Research Agreement forms to ELR@wellesley.edu in order to obtain a university signature. These documents are becoming increasingly common and Career Education has developed a new process for managing and fully executing the agreements. The Experiential Learning Requirement grants students the opportunity to earn degree-bearing credit for these experiences, which will be acknowledged as part of the form. I have questions about the Experiential Learning Requirement (ELR). Who do I ask? Please reach out to ELR@wellesley.edu with any general questions about this requirement. If you are seeking support in identifying opportunities, or want to discuss experiential learning possibilities, we encourage you to make an appointment in Handshake to meet with your Advisor for Career Exploration (ACE). Hayley Meredith is the ACE for the class years of 2028 and 2030. Ariane Baker is the ACE for the class years of 2027 and 2029. Can I earn experiential learning units while studying abroad? Wellesley College formal study abroad programs are academic credit-bearing and not eligible for experiential learning units. However, any experiential learning (internships, research, civic engagement)  self-identified by a student while abroad and outside of the academic program are eligible under the same guidelines as domestic opportunities. Students should submit a post-experience ELR application for these units in Workday. Let’s Talk Contact T. (781) 283-2352 careereducation@wellesley.edu Modulars 400 ( temporary for 2025-2026) 106 Central Street Wellesley, MA 02481 Career Education is available for virtual and in-person appointments . Employers: please visit our recruiting landing page and email recruiting@wellesley.edu with questions. Handshake Disclaimer & Employer Policies Copyright © Trustees of Wellesley College Terms of Use Privacy Policy Press Donate to the Program Handshake Hive Instagram Facebook Linkedin"
    },
    "https://careereducation.wellesley.edu/resources-parents-families": {
      "title": "Resources for Parents & Families",
      "description": "Resources for Parents & Families | Wellesley Career Education Skip to main content MyWellesley Give Handshake Hive Events ELR Getting Started Employers Faculty Parents About Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Resources for Parents & Families Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Handshake Hive Events Deadlines Resources Recruit About Home Resources for Parents & Families Resources for Parents & Families For many college students and their families, it is not long after a student decides where she will attend college that the question “What will she do after she graduates?” becomes a frequent topic of inquiry. Luckily, at Wellesley College, she is in the perfect place to begin to explore the interests, priorities, skills, and strengths that will help her to answer that question and prepare for the lifelong career journey ahead. How to Help Your Student Explore As a parent, you may feel anxiety as your student proceeds through college, eager for her to identify a particular path that will ensure both her happiness and her security when she graduates. Rest assured that some uncertainty and even changing her mind several times is an important part of the journey! College is not only the perfect time for a student to explore and identify her major interests, but also the time to develop the skills that employers cite as most important in their workforce. This resource is an overview of how you can support her in her journey. Learn More. Learn about our Model, Mission, & Technology Our About Us page provides an overview of our mission & technology, and introduces you to our four teams. Meet the dedicated Career Education staff, who work in collaboration with one another to help your student explore and connect her to opportunities and mentors. Read More Let’s Talk Contact T. (781) 283-2352 careereducation@wellesley.edu Modulars 400 ( temporary for 2025-2026) 106 Central Street Wellesley, MA 02481 Career Education is available for virtual and in-person appointments . Employers: please visit our recruiting landing page and email recruiting@wellesley.edu with questions. Handshake Disclaimer & Employer Policies Copyright © Trustees of Wellesley College Terms of Use Privacy Policy Press Donate to the Program Handshake Hive Instagram Facebook Linkedin"
    },
    "https://careereducation.wellesley.edu/careeradvising": {
      "title": "Susan L. Wagner ’82 Career Advising Program",
      "description": "Susan L. Wagner ’82 Career Advising Program | Wellesley Career Education Skip to main content MyWellesley Give Handshake Hive Events ELR Getting Started Employers Faculty Parents About Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Susan L. Wagner ’82 Career Advising Program Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Handshake Hive Events Deadlines Resources Recruit About Home Susan L. Wagner ’82 Career Advising Program The Susan L. Wagner ’82 Career Advising Program What do you want to be when you graduate? Rest assured that most Wellesley students are unsure about their career paths and even those that feel certain often do — and should! — change their course. When beginning your career journey, it can be tempting to imagine that it will be one straight line. However, as countless alums will tell you, that’s not the way it works — and that’s a good thing! As the Bureau of Labor Statistics reports, the average worker will have 12-15 jobs in 5-7 different career fields in her lifetime. Career Exploration Regardless of what year you are at Wellesley, we are here to help you explore potential career paths. Every student is matched with an Advisor for Career Exploration (ACE), who is available for conversations about your values, strengths, and interests. Your ACE will provide the mentorship, tools, connections, and space for self-assessment and reflection to identify the paths you want to pursue. Schedule an appointment in Handshake to meet with your ACE as follows: First Years & Juniors: Hayley Meredith, ACE for the Class of 2028 & 2030 Sophomores & Seniors: Ariane Baker, ACE for the Classes of 2027 & 2029 Career Advising for Specific Industries and Fields You may already have some inclination about what industry or field you might like to work within. You also may still be quite uncertain — and that is both normal and completely okay! You don’t need to know exactly what you want to do to meet with a specialized Career Advisor — simply having curiosity about a field or industry is a great place to begin. To meet with an industry/field specific Career Advisor, make an appointment in Handshake by selecting “Career, Internship, and Graduate School by Industry/Field.“ If you aren’t sure which Advisor covers your field of interest, email careereducation@wellesley.edu and we’ll point you in the right direction! The categories are broadly encompassing, so no matter what your career interests are, there is an Advisor who can work with you to explore the fields you are considering. Arts, Media, & Technology #amt Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing #bcfm Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ #cegia Pre-Law Advising #law Health Professions Advising #healthprofessions Science, Behavioral Health, & Public Health #sbp Students — Subscribe to Industry Newsletters Sign up for Industry newsletters by filling out your Career Interest profile in Handshake. You’ll receive advice specifically related to the industries of your interest, along with highlighted events, resources, jobs, internships, and fellowship opportunities in your field of interest. Subscribing to multiple newsletters is a great way to explore career options! Subscribe to Newsletters in Handshake Career Essentials: Resumes, Cover Letters, Interviews, & More! Through the Career Essentials workshop series, you will develop skills and the foundational knowledge necessary to navigate career development. Learn about resumes, cover letters, interviewing, informational interviewing, negotiation, and personal statements. Watch the series & explore resources Recruiting Timelines for Specific Industries and Fields Many industries have different recruiting schedules—learn about timelines and career paths, and explore your interests with the help of our industry/field specific Career Advisors, and through the drop-downs below! Arts, Media, Nonprofit, & Human Services Careers webpage (#amn) View Hiring Timelines Students can organize their search and prep their documents and portfolios (as needed) in the fall to prepare for spring hiring timelines. UI/UX and Product Design: Recruiting starts in January and ramps up by February, recruiting slows down by summer Publishing and Journalism : Recruiting for full-time publishing and journalism roles is between late December and May. Post-graduation internships or fellowships are common in these industries and can lead to a full-time position. Arts and Media in general: January - mid-April primarily. Nonprofit: Many nonprofit organizations will hire new graduates in the springtime because they need time to see what their needs and budget will be at that time.  In addition, they will start a hiring process when they have an opening which could occur anytime–often from early to late spring. Human Services: Because this field includes a variety of roles and organization, there is not a specific timeline.  Most organizations will likely have openings when someone leaves or a department is expanding.  They will start a hiring process close to the time when they would like to fill a role. Education, Public Health, & International Affairs Hiring Timelines webpage (#ephia) View Hiring Timelines Education: Those hoping to work in education as teachers or administrators will understand that most schools or educational organizations take the academic calendar into account.  Many schools will post opportunities for the fall in March or April.  That said, many schools will learn in August that they have openings, so hiring goes on even in late August and September. Public health research & clinical positions: usually hiring in Feb-April for summer starts. Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Timelines webpage (#bcfm) View Hiring Timelines Finance: The majority of financial services companies hire for full-time roles through their internship programs. Offers are typically extended in late summer. For other full-time hires, recruiting takes place from June through October. To be competitive at firms that recruit interns, applicants should apply directly on the firm’s website by the end of December of the previous year. Recruiting for financial services (in non-banking roles) occurs between September and January. Consulting: Most full-time roles are filled through the organization's summer internship programs with offers typically made in late summer. For other full-time hires, the recruiting cycle typically occurs in September through December/early January. Applicants should closely monitor company websites throughout the month of August. Marketing: Peak recruiting occurs between January and May with the exception of rotational programs. Advertising-specific marketing roles are recruited for during the months of February through May. Applicants should start reviewing company websites by early January. Government, Law, & Public Policy Timelines webpage (#glp) View Hiring Timelines Law: For seniors not pursuing law school but seeking legal jobs, most entry-level paralegal and legal assistant positions hire in mid/late spring. There are a few prestigious legal  opportunities with fall deadlines, including the Mintz Project Analyst Program and the Kobre & Kim Analyst program. Keep an eye on #GIALP newsletters for announcements about these earlier recruiting opportunities! Government: Most entry-level jobs in government are posted in the spring, and are looking for people to begin work immediately (so not looking to hire in January and then wait 6 months for the candidate to graduate). This is largely applicable to federal, state, and local hiring, but we encourage you to meet with Career Education to talk about your specific goals and drill down into possible differences between government agencies. For example, the Department of Justice  often posts entry-level paralegal roles staggered throughout the spring, so those aren't all technically for \"immediate hire\", and a great strategy is to set up a Saved Search for these opportunities on USAJobs in the fall. The most notable exception to the spring hiring rule is in the intelligence community, where the screening and background check process can take many months, so if those roles interest you, you would want to look into intelligence opportunities as early as possible (at least a year in advance) to learn the timelines for the specific intelligence agencies you are pursuing. Think Tanks: Some of the larger think tanks may post entry-level opportunities in the early spring semester (with deadlines as early as January), but this can vary from year to year and from employer to employer. Think tanks more often post entry-level opportunities in mid-late spring. Fellowships: Overlying all of this is fellowships, of course! There are many, many fellowships geared toward people pursuing careers in government and policy, so we encourage you to have fellowships on your radar this summer and fall, and then move on to applying for spring opportunities as needed after considering/applying for fellowships in the fall. Health Professions Advising webpage (#healthprofessions) View Timelines Healthcare: Recruiting timelines in healthcare can vary greatly depending on the type of opportunity (research, teaching, public health work, etc.) Opportunities in these areas are typically posted between February and May. Health Professions Schools: Detailed information about applying for Health Professions can be found on our website, here . Make sure to note our Deadlines and Requirements for a Committee Letter , download and read the applicant handbook and watch the Application Kickoff Video ! Science, Technology, & Engineering Careers webpage (#stem) View Hiring Timelines Science: Undergraduate hiring typically happens between January and May for research roles in non-profits, medical and research organizations. Technology: Entry level roles tend to be posted in the late summer through early September, and the recruiting cycle wraps up by the end of December. Mid sized and small tech firms usually begin recruiting in Jan/Feb, while tech start-ups often begin the process in Feb/March.  Non-tech roles at tech companies tend to be posted a bit later than the more technical roles at that company. Career Advisors Christopher De La Cerda Susan L. Wagner ’82 Director, Career Advising & Inclusive Excellence Initiatives Ariane Baker Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2027 & 2029) Layne Flynn Senior Assistant Director, Health Professions Advising Hebert Labbate Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs Hilda Lief Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Hayley Meredith Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2028 & 2030) Nicole D. Park Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Pre-Law Cindy Seltzer, Ed.D., MBA Helen Wallace ’33 Director, Health Professions Advising Alexis Trench Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Science, Behavioral Health, & Public Health Make an Appointment Embark: First Year Career Retreat Embark — a retreat for all interested first-year students — is a free weekend-long immersive program designed to equip you with a toolkit to begin your career exploration. Join us to connect with Career Education, discover how to identify and utilize your values and strengths through our assessments and activities, hear career stories from your Wellesley sibs, and learn about the variety of opportunities that are available to you as a Wellesley student, like funded internships, study abroad, and research. Learn More Peer Career Mentors Toluwani Ademola ’28 Peer Career Mentor Sarah Arambula-Sanchez ’26 Peer Career Mentor Efe Esemuze ’27 Peer Career Mentor Ilham Matiker ’27 Health Professions Peer Career Mentor Melissa Rizzo ’28 Peer Career Mentor Explore Employment Around the World with GoinGlobal GoinGlobal is the leading provider of both country-specific and USA city-specific career and employment information. Our unlimited access subscription database features 38 Country Career Guides, 53 City Career Guides for the United States and Canada, corporate profiles and more than 16 million internship and job listings within the USA and around the world. Wellesley's GoinGlobal Subscription is Accessible via Handshake Deadlines Events Jobs/Internships Resources Stories #accounting/insurance #advancement #amt #architecture #biotech/lifescience #careeredplacements #coverletter #exploring #faculty/staff #fellowshipstoolkit #highereducation #law #medschool #selfreflection #students #arts/design #bcfm #business/operations #careerpivot #datascience #employers #explore/try #government #gradschool #internshipgrants #interview #preparing #socialsciences #undergraduatefellowship #applying #cegia #consulting #encorecareer #engineering #entertainment #fundedinternship #gettingstarted #internationalaffairs #lawschool #linkedin #postgraduatefellowship #wellesleyopportunity #civicengagement #entrepreneurship #fundraising #industryexpertise #internationalstudent #k12education #law #negotiation #physicalscience #researchingoptions #distinctpopulations #finance #freelance #mentorship #networking #othereducation #publichealth #research/gradschool #sbp #understandingmyself #behavioralhealth #careerexploration #healthprofessions #management/hr #politics/advocacy #rejoiningtheworkforce #research #resume #servicefellowship #fellowships #marketing #media/pr #publishing #studyabroad #sustainability #travelfellowship #nonprofit #reflection #thinktank #professionalism #technology #writing/editing #recruit #socialmedia #careeressentials #socialscience #arts/humanities #science #careeradvancement #Resource Tags – Internships & Grants #Resource Tags - Faculty & Staff #Resource Tags - Recruiting #Other Tags - for grouping purposes only #Resource Tags - Pre-Law #Resource Tags - Communications, Education, Government, International Affairs #Resource Tags - Alumnae #Resource Tags - Science, Behavioral Health, Public Health #Resource Tags - Career Essentials & Exploration #Resource Tags - Health Professions #Resource Tags - Business, Consulting, Finance, Marketing #Resource Tags - Arts, Media, Technology #Resource Tags - Industry Advising #Resource Tags - Career Exploration #Resource Tags - Grad School #Resource Tags - Fellowships & Scholarships Popular Tags #careeressentials #amt #cegia #law #healthprofessions #sbp Entertainment Wellesley Career Education Today's entertainment industry includes everything from the large scale productions of the Hollywood studio system, to the independent work being done within smaller markets, to the new media offerings adapting at the fringe of established distribution channels. A lot of attention and publicity is given to the “above the line” careers such as directing, producing, writing, and acting, but just as many career opportunities are available within support industries like post-production, entertainment law, talent representation, public relations and marketing, and social media. Read More Tell us a little bit about the Goldwater Scholarship and what inspired you to apply. - Kennedy Barnes ’25 2024 Goldwater Scholarship for Chemistry Discounted Princeton Review Test Preparation Wellesley Career Education Wellesley College has a contract with the Princeton Review that allows all students to get a 50% discount on the below test prep courses. This includes the MCAT, GMAT, LSAT, GRE, and DAT. Read More Alumnae Career Conversation with Daena Title ’79 (Webinar, November 2023) More Expanding the Definition of Women in Technology: Career Chat with Cecilia Harvey ’00, Founder and Chair of Tech Women Today (Webinar, June 2023) More How to Sign up for Industry Newsletters Wellesley Career Education Update your Career Interests in Handshake to sign up for Industry-Specific Newsletters! Read More Load more Let’s Talk Contact T. (781) 283-2352 careereducation@wellesley.edu Modulars 400 ( temporary for 2025-2026) 106 Central Street Wellesley, MA 02481 Career Education is available for virtual and in-person appointments . Employers: please visit our recruiting landing page and email recruiting@wellesley.edu with questions. Handshake Disclaimer & Employer Policies Copyright © Trustees of Wellesley College Terms of Use Privacy Policy Press Donate to the Program Handshake Hive Instagram Facebook Linkedin"
    },
    "https://careereducation.wellesley.edu/career_exploration": {
      "title": "Career Exploration by Class year",
      "description": "Career Exploration by Class year | Wellesley Career Education Skip to main content MyWellesley Give Handshake Hive Events ELR Getting Started Employers Faculty Parents About Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Career Exploration by Class year Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Handshake Hive Events Deadlines Resources Recruit About Home Career Exploration by Class year Exploring careers? Start here! Regardless of what year you are at Wellesley, we are here to help you explore potential career paths. Every student is matched with an Advisor for Career Exploration (ACEs), who is available for conversations about your values, strengths, and interests. Your ACE will provide the mentorship, tools, connections, and space for self-assessment and reflection to identify the paths you want to pursue. Schedule an appointment in Handshake to meet with your ACE as follows: First Years & Juniors: Ariane Baker, ACE for the Classes of 2027 & 2029 Sophomores & Seniors: Hayley Meredith, ACE for the Class of 2028 & 2030 Below, we have broken down some of opportunities to get you started in thinking about your career journey. This is not intended to be a comprehensive checklist; rather, think of this as a collection of some of our most frequently referenced resources. Quick links: First Years • Sophomores • Juniors • Seniors Career Essentials: Resumes, Cover Letters, Interviews, & More! Through the Career Essentials workshop series, you will develop skills and the foundational knowledge necessary to navigate career development. Learn about resumes, cover letters, interviewing, informational interviewing, negotiation, and personal statements. Watch the series & explore resources The Wellesley Hive The Wellesley Hive is a digital platform exclusively for the Wellesley community where you can mentor or find mentorship and learn about possible career paths. The Hive is a platform where alumnae and students can ask questions and seek guidance, coach and inspire, and find paths to professional aspirations during one-time conversations or through more long-term mentorship. Explore the Hive Career Exploration Resources The following resources will can help you along your career exploration journey. These resources will be helpful as you attend career fairs, conduct research, identify members of your personal advisory board, and schedule informational interviews or job shadow opportunities. Making the Most of a Virtual Internship or Project 20+ Questions to Jump-start Your Career Exploration Assess Yourself: What Are You Bringing on Your Career Journey? Job Shadowing How to Conduct Career Research How to Use a Career Fair as an Exploration Opportunity Making the Most of a Virtual Internship or Project 20+ Questions to Jump-start Your Career Exploration Assess Yourself: What Are You Bringing on Your Career Journey? Job Shadowing How to Conduct Career Research How to Use a Career Fair as an Exploration Opportunity Hayley Meredith Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2028 & 2030) hm100@wellesley.edu Make an Appointment Resources for First Years Welcome to Wellesley! As a first-year student, your priority is to explore and try new things academically, personally, and professionally. This is a great time to learn more about yourself and your interests, discover new and exciting options for your future, and build community with your peers! Check out some of our resources below to get you started. But remember, these are just a handful of the opportunities and resources available to you, so keep exploring! You can schedule an appointment with me in Handshake to talk at any time. Select the “Career Exploration, Reflection, and General Guidance” appointment type. Join us for Embark — a First-Year Career Retreat 20+ Questions to Jump-start Your Career Exploration Assess Yourself: What Are You Bringing on Your Career Journey? Career Essentials Summer Hive Internship Projects Campus Research Science Center Summer Research Program Social Science Summer Research Program Knapp Fellows Program in the Social Sciences Wellesley Centers for Women Summer Research Ariane Baker Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2027 & 2029) as101@wellesley.edu Make an Appointment Resources for Sophomores Sophomore year is a great time to continue your career exploration by trying an internship, research program, or other experiential learning opportunity! This is also your year for major declaration and study abroad considerations, which we are always happy to discuss with you. Below are some suggested resources to help you explore different opportunities. Remember, these are just a handful of the opportunities and resources available to you, so keep exploring! You can schedule an appointment with me in Handshake to talk at any time. Select the “Career Exploration, Reflection, and General Guidance” appointment type. Learn about Career Essentials Don’t forget to explore our various Industry Advising resources , and to sign up for industry-specific newsletters by editing the “Career Interests” section in Handshake. Career Education Signature Internships Wellesley Grants Program Other College & Employer-Sponsored Internships McNair Scholars Program Mellon Mays Undergraduate Fellowship Program Study Abroad and resources from the Office of International Study Mentorship Affinity Pods (MAP) Program Campus Research Science Center Summer Research Program Sophomore Early Research Program Social Science Summer Research Program Knapp Fellows Program in the Social Sciences Wellesley Centers for Women Summer Research Hayley Meredith Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2028 & 2030) hm100@wellesley.edu Make an Appointment Resources for Juniors Junior year is an exciting time with lots of opportunities available to you for exploration, internships and research, and further reflection on how you might like your post-Wellesley life to look. I am here to help with it all! Below are some suggested resources to help you start to explore what’s possible in your junior year. Let’s be in touch any time to discuss these opportunities and more! You can schedule an appointment with me in Handshake to talk at any time. Select the “Career Exploration, Reflection, and General Guidance” appointment type. Don’t forget to explore our various Industry Advising resources , and to sign up for industry-specific newsletters by editing the “Career Interests” section in Handshake. Learn the Career Essentials Career Education Signature Internships Wellesley Grants Program Other College & Employer-Sponsored Internships Ready, Set, Senior: Resources and event specifically for juniors (aka soon-to-be seniors) Fellowships & Scholarships : Junior year is an essential time to explore the possibilities! Wellesley in Washington summer internship program The Albright Institute Mentorship Affinity Pods (MAP) Program Campus Research Science Center Summer Research Program Social Science Summer Research Program Knapp Fellows Program in the Social Sciences Wellesley Centers for Women Summer Research ​ Ariane Baker Susan L. Wagner ’82 Associate Director/Advisor for Career Exploration (Classes of 2027 & 2029) as101@wellesley.edu Make an Appointment Resources for Seniors Senior year can be an exciting and hectic time to navigate. From participating in special traditions, to soaking up final classes and rhythms of life at Wellesley, to taking steps toward pursuing whatever might come next, it can be tricky to balance it all. This is a great time to take advantage of the resources available to you through Career Education. Whether you’re hoping to attend graduate or professional school, conduct a job search, or find a gap year(s) opportunity (to name a few possible next steps), your ACE and the Industry Advisors are here to support you along the way. Our team is ready to walk alongside you as you prepare to transition to life after Wellesley, whether you have a clear sense of direction or still feel unsure about what to pursue next. Check out some of the resources below to start exploring what senior year has to offer. You can schedule an appointment with me in Handshake to talk at any time. Select the “Career Exploration, Reflection, and General Guidance” appointment type. Remember: after leaving Wellesley, you will still have access to Career Education advising for life as an alum ! Don’t forget to explore our various Industry Advising resources , and to sign up for industry-specific newsletters by editing the “Career Interests” section in Handshake. Mentorship Affinity Pods (MAP) Program Learn the Career Essentials Graduate school resources Fellowships & Scholarships Sign up for The Wellesley Hive to make connections with alums The Albright Institute Check out our fall event, Launch: A Guide to Life After College , and other resources for your life after Wellesley Let’s Talk Contact T. (781) 283-2352 careereducation@wellesley.edu Modulars 400 ( temporary for 2025-2026) 106 Central Street Wellesley, MA 02481 Career Education is available for virtual and in-person appointments . Employers: please visit our recruiting landing page and email recruiting@wellesley.edu with questions. Handshake Disclaimer & Employer Policies Copyright © Trustees of Wellesley College Terms of Use Privacy Policy Press Donate to the Program Handshake Hive Instagram Facebook Linkedin"
    },
    "https://careereducation.wellesley.edu/arts-media-technology": {
      "title": "Arts, Media, & Technology",
      "description": "Arts, Media, & Technology | Wellesley Career Education Skip to main content MyWellesley Give Handshake Hive Events ELR Getting Started Employers Faculty Parents About Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Arts, Media, & Technology Career Advising Career Exploration Arts, Media, & Technology Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ Health Professions Advising Pre-Law Advising Science, Public Health, & Behavioral Health Career Essentials Resources Drop-ins & Peer Career Mentors Graduate School Internships Policies Application Support Short Internship Projects (SHIPs) Wellesley-funded Internships & Grants FAQs Fellowships Fellowships for Undergraduates Fellowships for Graduates Class of 2027 Fellowship Planning Fellowships for International Students Fellowships Browser Civic Engagement Ministrare Peer Leader Office Hours Alumnae BEAM Entrepreneurship Advising Make It Make Cents Wellesley in Business Symposium Handshake Hive Events Deadlines Resources Recruit About Home Arts, Media, & Technology Arts, Media, & Technology Destiny M. Barletta Director, Alumnae Connections Christopher De La Cerda Susan L. Wagner ’82 Director, Career Advising & Inclusive Excellence Initiatives Many exciting and fulfilling careers exist across the spectrum of Arts, Media, & Technology, and Wellesley’s rigorous liberal arts curriculum prepares students and alumnae with strengths that are especially relevant in these fields. Whether you are interested in working in a museum, a start-up, the entertainment industry, Big Tech, or somewhere in-between, Career Education is here to support you in your search. Students and alumnae are encouraged to get started by exploring the resources below and scheduling an appointment in Handshake. Christopher De La Cerda is currently advising Tech students, and Destiny Barletta is advising student in the areas of Arts & Media. Arts Resources The resources below explore a broad range of careers in art and design; from architecture, to fashion, to museum work, and beyond. Architecture Arts Administration Arts Education Creative Writing Design and Illustration Fashion and Lifestyle Fine Art Museums and Galleries Performing Arts Paper Saver: Alumnae Career Conversation with Mary French ’09, Book Conservator (Recorded Talk, April 2018) Architecture Arts Administration Arts Education Creative Writing Design and Illustration Fashion and Lifestyle Fine Art Museums and Galleries Performing Arts Paper Saver: Alumnae Career Conversation with Mary French ’09, Book Conservator (Recorded Talk, April 2018) Media Resources Learn more about working in media, publishing, and entertainment industries. Entertainment Media Pathways in Production Alumnae Panel Discussion (May 2022) Entertainment Media Pathways in Production Alumnae Panel Discussion (May 2022) Technology Technology is present across all industries and disciplines, so the application of technical knowledge can be found in numerous career opportunities. Begin exploring through the resources below. Computer Science and Technology Technology Internships for First Years and Sophomores Alumnae Career Conversation: Anne Dickinson Meltz '16 (Wayfair); Elizabeth Good '09 (Wayfair); Hayden Broberg '14 (Insurify) (Webinar, May 2020) Computer Science and Technology Technology Internships for First Years and Sophomores Alumnae Career Conversation: Anne Dickinson Meltz '16 (Wayfair); Elizabeth Good '09 (Wayfair); Hayden Broberg '14 (Insurify) (Webinar, May 2020) Office hours Appointments can be scheduled in Handshake for Mon–Fri. Sign up for newsletters! Update your Career Interests in Handshake to sign up for Arts, Media, & Technology newsletters! You'll learn about events, discover opportunities, hear alumnae stories, and more. Deadlines Events Jobs/Internships Resources Stories #accounting/insurance #advancement #amt #architecture #biotech/lifescience #careeredplacements #coverletter #exploring #faculty/staff #fellowshipstoolkit #highereducation #law #medschool #selfreflection #students #arts/design #bcfm #business/operations #careerpivot #datascience #employers #explore/try #government #gradschool #internshipgrants #interview #preparing #socialsciences #undergraduatefellowship #applying #cegia #consulting #encorecareer #engineering #entertainment #fundedinternship #gettingstarted #internationalaffairs #lawschool #linkedin #postgraduatefellowship #wellesleyopportunity #civicengagement #entrepreneurship #fundraising #industryexpertise #internationalstudent #k12education #law #negotiation #physicalscience #researchingoptions #distinctpopulations #finance #freelance #mentorship #networking #othereducation #publichealth #research/gradschool #sbp #understandingmyself #behavioralhealth #careerexploration #healthprofessions #management/hr #politics/advocacy #rejoiningtheworkforce #research #resume #servicefellowship #fellowships #marketing #media/pr #publishing #studyabroad #sustainability #travelfellowship #nonprofit #reflection #thinktank #professionalism #technology #writing/editing #recruit #socialmedia #careeressentials #socialscience #arts/humanities #science #careeradvancement #Resource Tags – Internships & Grants #Resource Tags - Faculty & Staff #Resource Tags - Recruiting #Other Tags - for grouping purposes only #Resource Tags - Pre-Law #Resource Tags - Communications, Education, Government, International Affairs #Resource Tags - Alumnae #Resource Tags - Science, Behavioral Health, Public Health #Resource Tags - Career Essentials & Exploration #Resource Tags - Health Professions #Resource Tags - Business, Consulting, Finance, Marketing #Resource Tags - Arts, Media, Technology #Resource Tags - Industry Advising #Resource Tags - Career Exploration #Resource Tags - Grad School #Resource Tags - Fellowships & Scholarships Popular Tags #architecture #arts/design #entertainment #media/pr #technology Entertainment Wellesley Career Education Today's entertainment industry includes everything from the large scale productions of the Hollywood studio system, to the independent work being done within smaller markets, to the new media offerings adapting at the fringe of established distribution channels. A lot of attention and publicity is given to the “above the line” careers such as directing, producing, writing, and acting, but just as many career opportunities are available within support industries like post-production, entertainment law, talent representation, public relations and marketing, and social media. Read More Discounted Princeton Review Test Preparation Wellesley Career Education Wellesley College has a contract with the Princeton Review that allows all students to get a 50% discount on the below test prep courses. This includes the MCAT, GMAT, LSAT, GRE, and DAT. Read More Alumnae Career Conversation with Daena Title ’79 (Webinar, November 2023) More Expanding the Definition of Women in Technology: Career Chat with Cecilia Harvey ’00, Founder and Chair of Tech Women Today (Webinar, June 2023) More How to Sign up for Industry Newsletters Wellesley Career Education Update your Career Interests in Handshake to sign up for Industry-Specific Newsletters! Read More Entrepreneurship & Branding: Alumnae Career Conversation with Dawn Nadeau ’95 (Webinar, October 2023) More Load more Arts, Media, & Technology #amt Business, Consulting, Finance, & Marketing #bcfm Communication, Education, Government, & International Affairs​ #cegia Pre-Law Advising #law Health Professions Advising #healthprofessions Science, Behavioral Health, & Public Health #sbp Let’s Talk Contact T. (781) 283-2352 careereducation@wellesley.edu Modulars 400 ( temporary for 2025-2026) 106 Central Street Wellesley, MA 02481 Career Education is available for virtual and in-person appointments . Employers: please visit our recruiting landing page and email recruiting@wellesley.edu with questions. Handshake Disclaimer & Employer Policies Copyright © Trustees of Wellesley College Terms of Use Privacy Policy Press Donate to the Program Handshake Hive Instagram Facebook Linkedin"
